WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.880
TV is a whole lot better than it used to be.

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:09.920
Over the past decade or so, so-called "prestige
TV" has offered audiences superb acting, cinematic

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:15.220
style, unpredictable storylines, and an epic
scope never before seen on the small screen.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:20.300
Many of these shows are serious, sometimes
devastating dramas with important things to

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:20.800
say.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:25.420
But even brilliant productions like these
crank out plenty of ridiculous and hilarious

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:26.880
bloopers and outtakes.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:28.760
Breaking Bad

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:30.700
Child actors are unpredictable.

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:33.900
And the younger they are, the more unpredictable
they can be.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:38.660
But productions only have a limited time each
day to use babies, so they can't wait around

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:43.730
for when the baby "feels" like acting, especially
since they don't really know they're acting.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:44.620
Nor do they care.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:49.520
Take this Breaking Bad outtake, for example,
in which the infant playing Holly White makes

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.940
some very interesting "choices" for her character
…

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.260
"The kids, they, they, they are safe.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:55.480
Of course they are."

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.180
"I don't think she feels very safe."

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:02.850
Game of Thrones

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:07.840
In this blooper from HBO's fantasy epic Game
of Thrones, Kit Harington plays the corpse

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:13.000
of Jon Snow, his body laid out on a table
for what will ultimately be his spooky resurrection

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.000
from the dead.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.660
Snow is supposed to be naked in this scene,
with only a modest, genital-concealing garment

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.320
preventing everyone on set from seeing Little
Jon …

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:26.620
Deadwood

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.860
So many gag reels consist of actors forgetting
their lines and swearing up a storm.

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:36.760
But what happens on a show like HBO's gritty
western Deadwood, in which half of the dialogue

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:37.840
is already swearing?

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:39.840
The actors simply burst into song.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.640
Or at least that's what happens when apparent
Rolling Stones fan Ian McShane can't remember

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.160
what comes after a line that includes the
words "start me" …

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:52.140
"Don't start me … Don't start me?

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:52.860
Oh!

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:53.480
'Start me up!

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.700
And you start me up, you whip me up, you put
me down …'"

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:57.500
Sons of Anarchy

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.570
There's a lot of platonic male-on-male affection
on Sons of Anarchy, especially in the form

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:04.040
of back-slapping bro hugs.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:09.440
But while shooting one of the show's many
action sequences, actor Ryan Hurst overestimates

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:14.610
how far he needs to move and winds up unusually
close to his fellow gun-toting, cover-seeking

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.060
costar Charlie Hunnam …

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:23.200
"What do you want for Christmas?"

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:25.500
Hannibal

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:31.500
Bryan Fuller's artfully gory television adaptation
of Thomas Harris' Hannibal Lecter novels featured

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:36.450
many scenes of its central cannibalistic serial
killer intricately preparing gourmet meals

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:37.760
in his beautiful kitchen.

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:42.340
A lot of that was likely achieved through
the magic of editing, because in this blooper,

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:46.980
actor Mads Mikkelsen can't quite master the
show-offy knife skills that would be second

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:48.180
nature to the character ...

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:56.500
"...invented in the 19th century."

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:58.680
Criminal Minds

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.739
Despite the gruesome subject matter of Criminal
Minds, it looks like the cast and crew keep

00:03:03.739 --> 00:03:06.050
things pretty light and loose on set.

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:10.510
He's no longer with the show, but Thomas Gibson
seemed to have a pretty good time while he

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:14.670
was starring on the long-running CBS crime
procedural — like the time it took him a

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:17.300
comically long time to put a pair of rubber
gloves on …

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:25.640
"Y'know, your prostate is the, about the right
size for a man your age."

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:28.820
Law &amp; Order: SVU

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:33.819
When you're making a long-running crime series
about the most appalling of crimes, allowing

00:03:33.819 --> 00:03:38.230
yourself to laugh here and there on set is
a good way for actors to cope with the depressing

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:38.780
material.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:44.640
Case in point: former Law &amp; Order:SVU star
Christopher Meloni can't get over how funny

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:49.720
it is—and how utterly creepy he sounds—to
ask a suspect for something rather unusual

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:52.620
"Would you mind if we took a DNA swab from
inside your mouth?

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:55.020
Even I felt creepy."

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:59.120
"Would you mind if we took a DNA swab from
inside your mouth?

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:01.400
It'll help us — Even I, I felt creepy again."

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:02.800
ER

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:09.300
The hit NBC hospital drama ER made George
Clooney a superstar, and not long after, he

00:04:09.301 --> 00:04:12.019
became one of the biggest movie stars on the
planet.

00:04:12.019 --> 00:04:16.470
He also had a unique way of coming up with
some dialogue in the event that he forgot

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:19.729
a line while filming the incredibly fast-paced
show …

00:04:19.729 --> 00:04:20.729
"We're gonna try something.

00:04:20.729 --> 00:04:25.140
I want you to close your eyes, I want you
to think of — Think of me, with no clothes

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:25.640
on."

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:26.680
"Would you just shut up and kiss me?"

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:27.180
"Kiss me!"

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:28.080
Orphan Black

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:34.120
Tatiana Maslany plays many different clones
on Orphan Black, but they're all pretty different.

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:39.150
For example, Alison Hendrix is a high-strung
Type-A kind of person, Sarah Manning is a

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:44.240
self-sufficient and streetwise rogue, and
the Ukrainian clone Helena always seems to

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.229
have really bad things happen to her.

00:04:46.229 --> 00:04:51.080
In this scene, there's a scorpion that's supposed
to be crawling all over Helena, but Maslany

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:52.499
just can't keep it together …

00:04:52.499 --> 00:04:53.580
"Inside of …"

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:55.210
"It was perfect."

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:57.379
"That was so good!"

00:04:57.379 --> 00:04:58.469
"So good!"

00:04:58.469 --> 00:05:00.099
"I'm so sorry."

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:01.720
Thanks for watching!

00:05:01.729 --> 00:05:04.699
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:04.699 --> 00:05:07.360
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

